12. What must be running?

For Telegram webhook testing, two things must be running:

Terminal 1
ngrok http 5678
Terminal 2
$env:N8N_WEBHOOK_URL="https://YOUR-NGROK-URL"
n8n

Then:

Telegram → ngrok → n8n → Telegram Trigger

13. What happens if you close ngrok?

If you stop:

ngrok http 5678

the public HTTPS endpoint disappears.

Then:

Telegram
   ↓
❌ Cannot reach n8n

So ngrok needs to remain running while you're testing Telegram/webhooks.

14. What happens when you restart ngrok?
This is very important.

Your ngrok URL may change.

Today:
https://spore-childhood-kooky.ngrok-free.dev

After restarting ngrok, you may get a different URL.

For example:
https://another-name.ngrok-free.dev

If the URL changes, update:
$env:N8N_WEBHOOK_URL="https://NEW-NGROK-URL"
and restart n8n.

⭐ The 5 commands to remember

For future reference, these are the key commands:

Check ngrok
ngrok version
Start tunnel
ngrok http 5678
Set n8n public webhook URL
$env:N8N_WEBHOOK_URL="https://YOUR-NGROK-URL"
Verify it
echo $env:N8N_WEBHOOK_URL
Start n8n
n8n
⭐ Most important concept to remember

You don't use ngrok because n8n needs HTTPS to run.

Your normal n8n can continue running as:

http://localhost:5678

We use ngrok because an external service such as Telegram needs a publicly reachable HTTPS webhook.

So remember:

Local n8n + external webhook service → use a public HTTPS tunnel such as ngrok.

This same concept applies not only to Telegram, but also to many webhook-based integrations such as payment gateways, GitHub webhooks, Slack events, etc.

############################################################################################################

This URL:

https://spore-childhood-kooky.ngrok-free.dev

is the public HTTPS address provided by ngrok. Telegram uses that address to communicate with your locally running n8n.

The communication flow is:

                INTERNET
                   │
                   │ HTTPS
                   ▼
              Telegram
                   │
                   │
                   ▼
https://spore-childhood-kooky.ngrok-free.dev
                   │
                   │ ngrok tunnel
                   ▼
          http://localhost:5678
                   │
                   ▼
                  n8n
                   │
                   ▼
          Telegram Trigger
In simple words

When someone sends a message to your Telegram bot:

User
 ↓
Telegram
 ↓
ngrok public HTTPS URL
 ↓
your Windows PC
 ↓
n8n
 ↓
Telegram Trigger

So Telegram does not directly communicate with localhost:5678.

It communicates with:

https://spore-childhood-kooky.ngrok-free.dev

and ngrok forwards that request to:

http://localhost:5678
One small correction

The URL isn't really "Telegram's URL" or "n8n's URL."

It's ngrok's public tunnel URL, which acts as a bridge:

Telegram
   ↓
ngrok public URL
   ↓
your local n8n

That's why the URL must remain active while you're testing the Telegram webhook.

And this same concept is very useful to remember for your API/webhook work:

External service → Public HTTPS endpoint → Tunnel → Local application


